#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101614} :activity/id #uuid "1e401592-79bc-5a8c-b040-fe705dfe9110" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050544 :topic/id #uuid "525bb99e-c082-5a7b-a26b-369998ff4ac8" :topic/math-academy-id 1207} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102007 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to sketch a possible graph of a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f'(3)=0$\n- if $x \\in (-\\infty,3)$ then $f'(x)> 0$\n- if $x \\in (3,+\\infty)$ then $f'(x)<0$\n- $f''(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n\nWe summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow\\:\\:\\frown$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow\\:\\:\\frown$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph:\n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=3$, where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n- Since $x=3$ is a local maximum, the graph must go down to the left and to the right of that point.\n- Finally, the graph must be concave down everywhere.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7c6d8412e9c657e8e4a148e40ca0b8a073735d3b88681eec6a79f763471aaf4f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7f1b863e-e172-5e84-8f8d-1ea21025605d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a979657-a144-5dbc-997b-c1dd2371aada" :step/math-academy-id 1253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100642 :question/id #uuid "744696c5-253d-5254-b7cc-d5dbcac566b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-341"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa143f7f-c07f-55dd-8907-d8b01ef8f418" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101205} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218650